    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[30, 9, 13, 10], [20, 25, 5, 27], [12, 11, 29, 7], [12, 8, 6, 12]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 3, 0, 1], [2, 3, 1, 0], [3, 2, 0, 1], [1, 3, 0, 2]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated Scheduling of Multi\u2011Step Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\n\nTeam,\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. We have several distinct production jobs to process, each made up of a fixed sequence of operations that must pass through specific machines. Each operation has a known run time, and the mix of routings and durations varies by job.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe core challenge is to sequence all required operations across our machines so that every job advances through its steps without violating capacity limits or operation order. Because different operations compete for the same machines at different times, and run times are not uniform, poor sequencing can create bottlenecks on critical machines while leaving others idle. This directly impacts throughput, lead times, and delivery reliability.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Fixed routing by job: Operations within the same job must be executed in the prescribed order; downstream steps cannot start until upstream steps finish.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation must be performed on its designated machine only, for its known processing duration.\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; there is no overlap or sharing of capacity.\n- No preemption: Once an operation begins, it must run to completion without interruption or splitting.\n\nWhat Success Looks Like\nOur aim is to produce a feasible, conflict\u2011free production plan that:\n- Starts each operation at a time that respects both machine availability and the job\u2019s internal sequence.\n- Balances load across machines to reduce idle time and waiting between steps.\n- Completes all jobs in the shortest possible overall time (minimizes makespan), while supporting on\u2011time delivery and efficient utilization.\n\nNext Steps\n- Confirm the operation sequences, required machines, and run times for each job.\n- Build a production calendar that assigns start/finish times to each operation by machine, respecting the rules above.\n- Review the resulting schedule for bottlenecks and refine sequencing as needed to shorten the overall completion time without violating constraints.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of Jobs: 4\n- Number of Machines (Workstations): 4\n- Machine Assignments by Operation: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 3\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 1]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 1, Op4: Machine 0]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 3, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 1]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 2]\n- Processing Times by Operation: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 4 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 30, Op2: 9, Op3: 13, Op4: 10]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 20, Op2: 25, Op3: 5, Op4: 27]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 12, Op2: 11, Op3: 29, Op4: 7]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 12, Op2: 8, Op3: 6, Op4: 12]",
